  "text": "arsimp to solve this problem, we will need to use some concepts related to geometry and trigonometry. Here are the steps we will follow:\n\n1. Identify the given information.\n2. Set up a right triangle using the given information.\n3. Apply the Pythagorean theorem to find the distance between the center of the sphere and the point where the wire touches the plane.\n4. Use trigonometry to find the height of the cylinder.\n5. Apply the Pythagorean theorem again to find the radius of the cylinder's base.\n\nLet's start with step 1. We are given the following information:\n\n- The angle made by the wire with the plane is 60 degrees.\n- The sphere is 10 cm away from the point where the wire touches the plane.\n- The radius of the sphere is 2 cm.\n\nNow, let's proceed to step 2. We will set up a right triangle using the given information. The wire is the hypotenuse, and we can denote the distance between the center of the sphere and the point where the wire touches the plane as \"a\". We can use trigonometry to find the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe know that:\n\n```\n    angle = 60 degrees\n    sphere's radius = 2 cm\n    sphere's distance from the wire's point of contact = 10 cm\n```\n\nUsing trigonometry, we can find the height of the cylinder:\n\n```\n    height = a * sin(angle)\n    height = a * sin(60 degrees)\n    height = a * (√3 / 2)\n```\n\nNow, let's move to step 3. We will use the Pythagorean theorem to find the distance \"a\". We know that:\n\n```\n    sphere's radius squared + sphere's distance squared = a squared\n    2 squared + 10 squared = a squared\n    4 + 100 = a squared\n    a squared = 104\n    a = √104\n```\n\nLastly, we will apply the Pythagorean theorem again to find the radius of the cylinder's base. The wire is also the hypotenuse in this right triangle, so its length will be (√3 ∙ √104). According to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n```\n    a squared + cylinder's base squared = height squared\n    (√104) squared + height squared = (√3 ∙ √104) squared\n    104 + height squared = 300\n    300 - 104 = height squared\n    196 = height squared\n    height = √196\n    height = 14 cm\n```\n\nSince the height of the cylinder is 14 cm and it is the diameter of the sphere's base (because when a sphere is placed on another sphere for a contactless placement, the difference in their radii equals the height of the cylinder when it rotates around the common base), we can find the radius of the cylinder's base:\n\n```\n    radius of the sphere's base = radius of the cylinder's base\n    radius of the sphere's base = 14 / 2\n    radius of the sphere's base = 7 cm\n```\n\nTherefore, the radius of the cylinder's base is 7 cm, and the final answer is \\boxed{7}.",
